==Question #1==
{{Cquote2|Can you explain how the requirements worked? It says 20 wilderness kills or an LMS win. According to my collection log I've only played 8 games of LMS with 3 kills and 0 wins. And I don't think I've even killed a single person in the wilderness. But I was able to vote on all the questions?}}

[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bpVigcSCZ0w&t=515s Youtube Time Stamp]

* '''Mod Ayiza:''' Essentially, I pretty much just got the details wrong in the blog, so apologies on my behalf! So the criteria was 20 kills on PvP worlds or within the Wilderness (that also includes Bounty Hunter as well I believe), or one LMS win - that was actually that you just had to have participated in an LMS game. So if you've done LMS, you were more than likely able to vote and that's probably how you managed to get in on the vote!
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' In the [https://secure.runescape.com/m=news/a=12/the-pvp-arena-poll-summary-blog?oldschool=1 PvP Arena Rewards summary blog], we go into a little bit of detail as to why we looked at restricting, and why we were quite lax on the restrictions. So I'd recommend checking that out for more info to save me going go for it now. But hopefully that answers your question!
* '''Mod Kirby:''' Just to add to that, I'm not particularly worried about the fact that we got the criteria wrong, because from the looks of the data that is also presented in the blog, most of the rewards would have failed anyway, even if we had the restrictions tighter. So it's something we could take as a learning point going forwards - maybe we can restrict tighter, maybe we can restrict looser. Either way, these rewards were not "there".
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' Yeah, I mean it had an impact, but it still wouldn't have been enough of an impact to have been the ultimate cause of the rewards failing, which is why we're looking at reworking them. But there are some questions on that later on that we'll get to!

==Question #2==
{{Cquote2|There were changes made to these rewards based on community feedback prior to polling, and they still failed the poll. Will you adjust how feedback is collected and used for changes made to these rewards?}}

[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bpVigcSCZ0w&t=630s Youtube Time Stamp]

* '''Mod Ayiza:''' Yes. In that same blog that we just mentioned, we talked about a [https://discord.gg/mpCAfTARmS PvP feedback Discord] that we've set up, that is now live. In the blog you'll see a link to be able to join that. Anyone is able to join that server in read-only, but we have restricted discussion to a select few members that filled out the survey when we first said we were doing feedback.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' What we're going to be doing over the course of the next few weeks is looking at if there are ways that we can either 1. rework the current rewards that we have, and their set effects and their stats, so that they're a little bit more favourable to everyone, or 2. looking at any alternative ideas for rewards that may utilise armour slots that we've already got some assets for that would also be fitting. We're going to handle that by initially gathering the suggestions from the PvP feedback Discord, and then creating a blog based off what's discussed there, and then sharing that to the wider player base as we would with any other blog.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' Essentially, what we're looking at doing is just having an extra step of validation for PvP specifically. We are pretty good when it comes to things such as skilling, and PvM, and quests. They're all very well established within the community and we know exactly who we want to be looking at if we want very specific and focused feedback. It's not so easy for PvP. There's so many sub-communities within there, so many different opinions.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' So we're going to use that as a way to validate any changes that we make, similar to how we would internally, and then post our blog and go through the normal feedback process of gathering feedback from the wider community. We'll then probably talk about that feedback again within the feedback Discord before we look at repolling. That's how we're adjusting the way that we collect feedback for PvP stuff, and these rewards specifically.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' We're not being really precious over the rewards that are there. We put out what we thought a section of PvPers wanted. We're not "trying to force the rewards to come back in", it's just that we have them there and it's an area that we can talk about. If we're able to rework them great. If not, then we'll just make new rewards.
* '''Mod Kirby:''' I would like to force the art in though, because the art for the armours is really nice in-game. I want them to be used!
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' This is one of the things that happens with the way we handle content and the fact that we've got the poll system. Historically, people have wanted an idea of what the armours or weapons might look like before they get added into the game and before they're voted on. But that does come with a risk that if it was to fail, then it might have been time spent on something that won't make it. We have that now at least, so in the future if we ever look at armours, we've got those to fall back on.

==Question #3==
{{Cquote2|Could the arena still be released and have the completed rewards come out separately? This would at least allow for people to play the arena and give adequate feedback on the initial launch as you guys are updating the rewards.}}

[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bpVigcSCZ0w&t=840s Youtube Time Stamp]

* '''Mod Kirby:''' Theoretically, we could just remove the shop in its entirety. That would probably just work, turning off reward points as well, that's also something I think we can reasonably do. So if everything fails then yeah, sure, we could still release it. The problem then is - is there much point in doing it if there's no payout? This is something we've been discussing internally as well. Having this PvP Arena with its ranking system is nice, but is there an intrinsic reason to play it? And does that maintain a player base for long enough the people are around to play it? Because there is a sort of "dead game effect" where players don't think they're going to get the game, so they weren't queue for the game and the population continually decreases, and it just sort of dies a slow and pitiful death. I'm hoping we're going to avoid this, but it's something that's worrying me at least!
* '''Mod Tide:''' I'd probably still let them get their points. You are going to have the rewards eventually, and you don't want to have people play all this time and then they haven't got anything to show for it.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' If we were going to do that, it's obviously a discussion point to have, I'm more in favour of what you've said, Tide - you can still gain your reward points to spend at some point. But at the same time, there'd be no point having reward points if there weren't any rewards coming out, period.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' One thing I can say on this, though, is that the Arena itself is something that we said from the start, we wouldn't be polling. So the PvP Arena will come out even if there aren't any rewards for it. If we do get to that point where we can't rework them or find anything that's suitable to be added to it, we may well be in a position where the Arena does come out without any rewards, at least initially. We might spend some more time looking at adding some in the future, but the Arena itself will will be coming regardless as the replacement to the Duel Arena.

==Question #4==
{{Cquote2|The high barrier to entry of making some sort of PVP account has been brought up before as something that deters people from wanting to get into PVP. However, adding equipment that can only be used in PVP serves to fracture the game, and gives the perception that PVP is a separate game mode. Do you believe that the correct approach involves separating the reward structure of the two communities? Or do you think it will worsen the predator/prey problem, because anyone that's killing wildly bosses, or something non-PK is unable to use the new BIS or near BIS equipment that their stacker is likely to be wearing?}}

[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bpVigcSCZ0w&t=985s Youtube Time Stamp]

* '''Mod Kirby:''' It's hard, because any item that's good in PvP is likely to be good in not-PvP as well. So if you want an item to be good, but just good for PvPers with the lower restrictions to get more access into PvP, we do kind of need to limit it. To the last point, there is technically nothing stopping you from bringing the PvP-only equipment as a switch, although I understand that is inventory spaces that you're bringing to not to fight the bosses, or whatever it is you're doing in the Wilderness, so that's a really awkward one. It's such a hard problem to solve!
* '''Mod Tide:''' One of the ideas I came up with was, we have normal stats, but for stuff we want to add to PvP we could have separate "PvP stats". If you're in a PvP zone, for instance, it will use those stats instead. Then we could balance it around those, and we wouldn't have to worry about PvE.
* '''Mod Kirby:''' The problem is that builds up to this same situation where PvP does exist as a separate entity to PvM and the rest of the game.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' Historically as well, we've had a problem with showing how stats are different in different zones. The interface for gear as it stands isn't great, in all honesty. My take on this though, I really, really dislike the fact that gear works differently in PvP and non-PvP. My preference would be that we would avoid it entirely and items would just be items that can be used. If they have benefit in PvP, great! If they just so happen to have benefit in PvM as well, also great!
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' The problem with that is you've then suddenly got items coming from a game mode that, quite frankly, a large portion of the playerbase don't really want to engage in. This brings forward that discussion point of feeling "forced" - it is a choice, by the way, when you enter the Wilderness. That choice is agreed upon when you step over the ditch or accept the warning on a teleport. But that's besides the point, because that sentiment is still there.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' So the whole "having items that are very specific to PvP" is our answer to that problem. If they don't have a use outside of PvP and you're not interested in PvP, then there is no reason that you would feel forced into having to get those items. It's not a great system, I'd rather that we didn't have to approach it that way. But from all of the negativity that we see around certain items that are only obtainable from the Wilderness that have uses outside of it, I don't really see another viable option for us to have.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' So I wouldn't say I see them as separate game modes. They're just very different communities with different opinions on what should and shouldn't happen, and we have to just try and accommodate that as best as we can.

==Question #5==
{{Cquote2|It seems that players don’t like the idea of restricted polls, will this change you might consider polling in the future?}}

[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bpVigcSCZ0w&t=1234s Youtube Time Stamp]

* '''Mod Ayiza:''' Alright, I'll say something that's probably going to get some backlash...
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' To me, it seems that players don't like the idea of restricted polls when it doesn't suit them. We've done restricted polls in the past for Ironmen, Group Ironmen, Ultimate Ironmen. There's never been any negativity towards that. It's always been seen as "oh, it's really nice that you're considering that community, it's really nice that you're looking to give them that extra support". Whilst the PvP Arena rewards one was a massive jump up in terms of how much we'd restricted it and how many questions you could vote on (and that was admittedly something that we should have spoke about in more detail before we did it). Even with that aside, this is the only time we've done it where we've seen some form of backlash where people have wanted to have their say.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' I understand why, as well. If you're someone who's in the Wilderness frequently and you've got someone who is attacking you, you might (rightly and fairly) want to be able to have a say on whether or not armours that help them kill you faster should be added into the game or not. But that whole basis is really only around - I don't want to say the "spite voting" side of things. But really, you are just saying you'd want to vote no because you don't want that person to have a better time in-game, because it makes your time in-game not as good.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' So how do we really combat that situation, other than doing restricted polling? You're never going to want to vote for something that will potentially make your experience in the game worse than it is. The only reason that mentality exists, in my opinion anyway, is because of this mindset of "I feel like I have to go to the Wilderness and when I'm in the Wilderness, I don't want to be spending my time there, I want to get out as quickly as possible".
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' If that mentality shifted to where you accepted the risks that you take when you go in there, it's really the only area left in the entire game where you can't predict everything. You can't go in there fully setup in maximum efficiency, where you know exactly what's going to happen, how long you're going to be able to stay there for, when you're going to have to go to the bank and how long it takes you to get back and what exact rates you're going to get. It's unpredictable. That's got quite a lot of charm to it, which is something that we're trying to protect and keep in the game, because it is so unique to Old School.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' A lot of players didn't like the idea of restricted polling. Will it change how we might consider it in the future? Absolutely. Will it stop us from doing it going forward? I don't think so. It was an experiment and I think it will continue to be experimental for us. There's nothing to say that we won't look at doing restricted polling in the future for things like PvM or skilling, should the questions warrant that level of restriction for example. It just so happens to be we had something very ready for PvP at the time, so that was the first one that got it.

